# THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt is generated and then compared byte-for-byte by
# `make verify-third-party-notices`, so git must round-trip it unchanged.
#
# `-text` — not `text eol=lf`. The generator itself writes LF, but it embeds
# third-party licence texts verbatim, and some of those carry CRLF of their
# own. Under `text eol=lf` git strips those CRs on the way into the blob, so
# the committed file can never byte-match a freshly generated one: the check
# fails on every clean checkout (CI, and the release preflight of both stages)
# while passing locally, where the working-tree file is still the one the
# generator wrote. `-text` disables eol conversion in both directions, which
# also covers the Windows `core.autocrlf` case the previous rule was aiming at.
THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt -text

# `.fvmrc` is the same class of file: fvm rewrites it in place unless it is
# already byte-identical to what fvm itself would emit, so it is committed in
# fvm's own serialization (its key order, LF, and no trailing newline). fvm
# writes it with Dart's `JsonEncoder.withIndent('  ')` + `writeAsStringSync`,
# which emits LF on every platform and performs no eol translation.
#
# Without `-text`, a Windows checkout under the default `core.autocrlf=true`
# lands CRLF in the working tree, so the file can never match fvm's output and
# every `fvm install` — which `make codegen` triggers, twice — rewrites it.
# git would still report the tree clean (it normalizes CRLF away on diff), so
# the churn is silent, and the invariant would hold only on Unix.
.fvmrc -text
